<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry the Bearded</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1165–<a href="/years/1238">1238</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Henry I the Bearded</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry the Bearded (c. 1165–1238) was Duke of Silesia from 1201 and, from 1232, High Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> as ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. With his wife Saint Hedwig he invited German settlers, founded towns under German law and endowed monasteries such as the Cistercian convent at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trzebnica">Trzebnica</a>, and he brought much of Poland under his rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-the-bearded.jpg" alt="Henry the Bearded" width="320" height="492" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolaus of Prussia (attributed) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HenrytheBearded.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was the fourth son of Duke Bolesław I the Tall of Silesia and his second wife Christina, probably a German. He was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>. His three older brothers died, and he became his father's sole heir. He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedwig-of-silesia">Hedwig of Andechs</a>, later canonised as Saint Hedwig of Silesia. Bolesław died on 8 December 1201, and early in 1202 Henry's uncle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-iv-tanglefoot">Mieszko IV Tanglefoot</a>, took the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a>, which had passed to Henry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Henry spent much of his rule contesting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-lebus">Lubusz Land</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. After the margrave Konrad II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> died on 6 May 1210 he took Lubusz and held it until 1218. On 23 November 1227, at an assembly of Piast dukes at Gąsawa, Henry and the High Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-white">Leszek I the White</a> were ambushed; Leszek was killed and Henry badly wounded. In 1229 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I of Masovia</a> had him seized at Spytkowice during mass and imprisoned at Płock, and he was freed only after Hedwig went to Płock and he renounced his rights to Kraków, a promise the Pope later released him from.</p><p>Władysław III of Greater Poland died in 1231 without a son, leaving Henry his heir. In 1232 Henry entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and was proclaimed High Duke, recovering for the Silesian Piasts the title lost by his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-the-exile">Władysław II the Exile</a> in 1146. A treaty with Konrad at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelm">Chełm</a> in 1233 confirmed his rule at Kraków. In 1234 he named his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious">Henry II the Pious</a> heir, as duke of Silesia and Greater Poland, and sought a royal crown for him, but conflict with the Church and his death ended the effort.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Henry supported mining, carried out a monetary reform and encouraged German settlers, mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. Złotoryja received the first town charter in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, in 1211, and he founded the Cistercian church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trzebnica">Trzebnica</a> in 1202 at his wife's request. He died in 1238 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krosno-odrzanskie">Krosno Odrzańskie</a> and was buried there. Historians agree that without the disaster at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> in 1241 Poland might have been united by the middle of the 13th century. A contemporary chronicler called him an honest man who thought only of being useful to his people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry the Bearded” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At a council of Polish Piast dukes near Gąsawa in Kuyavia, attackers assassinated Leszek the White, High Duke of Poland, and gravely wounded Duke <b>Henry the Bearded</b> of Silesia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-24">24 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henry I the Bearded</b> and his wife Saint Hedwig invited German settlers, founded towns under German law and monasteries such as Trzebnica.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Duchy of Silesia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krosno-odrzanskie">Krosno Odrzańskie</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.66,16.08&amp;year=1212" aria-label="Głogów on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4913 376 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry the Bearded"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5363 646h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5363 646h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.66,16.08&amp;year=1212">What was here in 1212? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
